 <section class="three-tool-section" id="countdown-meaning"><h3>Does Ramadan start at midnight, sunset or Fajr?</h3>
  <p>In Islamic datekeeping, a new Hijri date begins at sunset. The first Ramadan <strong>night</strong> therefore precedes the first Ramadan <strong>daytime fast</strong>. Fasting itself begins at true dawn, described in <a href="https://quran.com/2/187" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Quran 2:187</a>; it does not begin at sunset the night before.</p>
  <p>A countdown to midnight is easy to program but does not mark the religious beginning of the month. This tool instead calculates Maghrib on the Gregorian evening <em>before</em> your selected first fast. The clock updates regularly while open, and after the calculated moment passes it says so rather than displaying a negative countdown.</p>
 </section>
 <section class="three-tool-section" id="countdown-start"><h3>Why is the expected first day of Ramadan uncertain?</h3>
  <p><a href="https://sunnah.com/bukhari:1909" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Sahih al-Bukhari 1909</a> connects the beginning and ending of the Ramadan fast with the crescent and describes completing Sha'ban when the sky is obscured. Communities differ over local and wider moon-sighting criteria or calculated-calendar policies. An <strong>Umm al-Qura</strong> date is useful as a working estimate, but is not a report of a locally observed crescent.</p>
  <p>Never read the countdown's default as a binding date for every country. Keep the expected first fast editable, and update it when the local authority you follow announces the month. The calendar's end also depends on whether Ramadan contains 29 or 30 days and the Shawwal announcement.</p>
  <p>For a fuller explanation of calendar conventions, see the <a href="/tools/hijri-converter/">Hijri Converter</a>, <a href="/tools/ramadan-timetable/">Ramadan Timetable</a> and <a href="/guides/ramadan-dates-and-timetables/">Ramadan dates guide</a>.</p>
 </section>
 <section class="three-tool-section" id="countdown-steps"><h3>How to use the Ramadan countdown, step by step</h3>
  <ol class="three-tool-steps"><li><strong>Enter your expected first fast.</strong> Use the Gregorian date of the first daytime fast, not the evening of a moon-sighting announcement.</li><li><strong>Choose your location.</strong> Search a city or enter both coordinates and its correct IANA timezone.</li><li><strong>Check the method.</strong> A calculated Maghrib is an astronomical estimate, and special polar/high-latitude treatment requires local guidance.</li><li><strong>Read the two events.</strong> The countdown targets the preceding sunset; the next morning’s Fajr is displayed separately when available.</li><li><strong>Recheck the announcement.</strong> Use the earlier/later date scenarios only as possibilities, then set the real date when confirmed.</li><li><strong>Export responsibly.</strong> An .ics event contains the calculated sunset instant, but will not automatically update after an announcement.</li></ol>
 </section>
 <section class="three-tool-section" id="countdown-example"><h3>Worked example: first fast on 8 February 2027</h3>
  <div class="three-tool-example"><p>Suppose a person types <strong>Monday, 8 February 2027</strong> as an <em>illustrative</em> first fasting day. The countdown targets the calculated sunset of <strong>Sunday, 7 February 2027</strong> at the chosen location. The first daytime fast begins at Fajr on 8 February.</p><p>We deliberately do not assign a universal sunset clock time here: Maghrib varies by city, timezone and local horizon. This example is <strong>not</strong> an official 2027 moon-sighting announcement.</p></div>
  <p>If the community later announces the first fast on 9 February, the relevant first evening moves to sunset on 8 February. Update the date in the tool; an earlier calendar download must be replaced manually.</p>
 </section>
 <section class="three-tool-section" id="countdown-methods"><h3>How does a Ramadan countdown handle location, DST and high latitudes?</h3>
  <p>The underlying <a href="https://github.com/batoulapps/adhan-js" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Adhan JS library</a> calculates prayer-time instants from geographical coordinates and method parameters. The countdown uses the resulting <strong>absolute UTC sunset instant</strong> and formats it in your selected IANA timezone. This avoids assuming your device uses the same clocks as Makkah, London or another chosen city.</p>
  <p>The countdown measures elapsed time to the instant, so daylight-saving transitions are naturally included. A printed local wall time can change under BST or other regional rules, but the UTC instant remains definite for the chosen settings.</p>
  <p class="three-tool-caution">A calculated sunset is not a direct horizon observation. At polar latitudes and in unusual twilight, an approximation may be unavailable or religiously disputed. The tool displays an unavailable result if it cannot establish a valid local sunset; it does not invent a substitute.</p>
 </section>
 <section class="three-tool-section" id="countdown-prep"><h3>What should I prepare before the first Ramadan fast?</h3>
  <ul><li>Confirm the announced month start and identify the first daytime fasting date.</li><li>Check the <a href="/tools/fasting-times/">Suhoor and Iftar times</a> for the location where you will actually fast, using the method followed by your mosque.</li><li>Review the difference between Fajr and an optional earlier imsak preparation reminder. The actual daily fast is tied to true dawn.</li><li>Prepare a manageable plan for Quran reading, charity, prayer and rest without treating a personally chosen target as a compulsory religious quota. The <a href="/tools/quran-planner/">Quran Planner</a> can help.</li><li>Use the <a href="/tools/laylatul-qadr-nights/">last ten nights planner</a> later in Ramadan, and recheck the length of the month as Shawwal approaches.</li></ul>
  <p><a href="https://quran.com/2/185" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Quran 2:185</a> places Ramadan in the context of Quranic revelation and fasting, with relevant qualifications for personal circumstances. This page is a calendar utility, not a decision tool for exemptions.</p>
 </section>
